"""Summarize completed CT geometry, independent-cell dynamics and cis controls."""
from pathlib import Path
import hashlib,html,json,shutil,zipfile
import pandas as pd
from ct_validation_delivery import HERE,OUT,STYLE,manifest
BASE=HERE/'outputs';ATLAS=BASE/'ct_peak_atlas_v1';FLOW=BASE/'ct_flow_noise_v1';CIS=BASE/'ct_cis_validation_v1'

def report():
 OUT.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
 d=pd.read_csv(FLOW/'metrics.csv');sel=(d['dataset']=='cell_split')&(d['transform']=='logstate_linear_rate')&(d['method']=='knn_30')&(d['stratum']=='all')
 q=d[sel&d.target.isin(['Astate_Bchange','Bstate_Achange'])].copy()
 q['Interval (hpf)']=q.start_hpf.astype(int).astype(str)+'–'+q.end_hpf.astype(int).astype(str)
 t=q.pivot(index='Interval (hpf)',columns='target',values='improvement_vs_interval_mean')*100
 t.columns=['A-state / B-rates: error reduction (%)','B-state / A-rates: error reduction (%)']
 cs=q.groupby('Interval (hpf)').mean_cosine.mean();t['Mean direction cosine (two directions)']=cs
 t.to_csv(OUT/'hybrid_independent_cell_summary.csv')
 c=pd.read_csv(CIS/'cis_validation_metrics.csv');cm=c.query('alpha==0.6 and scope=="connected_at_current_threshold"').groupby(['threshold','method']).improvement_vs_temporal.mean().unstack()*100
 cm['cis_minus_shrinkage_percentage_points']=cm['cis']-cm['degree_matched_shrink'];cm.to_csv(OUT/'cis_conditional_summary.csv')
 cov=pd.read_csv(CIS/'cis_graph_coverage.csv')[['threshold','undirected_edges','peaks_with_edges','fraction_all_peaks_with_edges']];cov['Atlas peaks connected (%)']=cov.pop('fraction_all_peaks_with_edges')*100
 cisbody='''<h1>Cis co-accessibility: separate conditional smoothing test</h1><p>The available fixed graph supports a small targeted analysis. At score ≥0.25, 4,525 peaks (0.706% of the atlas) have an edge. Smoothing therefore cannot act as an atlas-wide regularizer with this graph.</p>'''+cov.to_html(index=False,float_format=lambda x:f'{x:.4g}')+'''<img src="00_cis_graph_coverage.png"><h2>Does coupling improve rate estimation?</h2><p>Cells were divided into balanced halves within celltype × stage × dataset. The same six-stage temporal model was fit separately to each half, then its rates were compared with raw rates in the other half. We tested α=1, 0.6, 0 and cutoffs 0.25, 0.5, 0.8, including self-weight 1. Each cutoff is reported on its own connected subset; their populations differ. Primary rates use the 21 celltypes adequately sampled at all six stages.</p><img src="01_cis_independent_cell_validation.png"><p>The table gives the arithmetic mean percentage error reduction versus the source-half temporal fit, across five intervals and two half directions (α=0.6). It is not a pooled-error percentage or a confidence interval.</p>'''+cm.to_html(float_format=lambda x:f'{x:.3f}')+'''<p>At 0.25, true cis coupling gives 1.23% average error reduction, versus 0.90% from self-weight-matched shrinkage: only 0.34 percentage points additional improvement. Pooling squared errors instead of averaging percentage reductions gives 0.99% for cis coupling. The 0.8 subset contains only 20 peaks and is unstable between half directions. Cutoffs 0.05 and 0.1 were audited for graph coverage only, not predictive performance.</p><h2>Controls and scope</h2><ul><li>The graph was learned from all cells. This is conditional validation of a fixed graph, not independently learned graph validation.</li><li>The genomic control matches chromosome, distance bins, edge-draw count and weighted row mass. Sampling with replacement reduces distinct degree for 767/4,525 targets at 0.25; 66/17,550 draws required a distance-bin fallback. It does not match abundance or neighbor indegree. It should not be treated as a perfect biological null.</li><li>The separate <code>degree_matched_shrink</code> table label refers to matched effective self-weight. This control replaces neighbor information by the source-half mean rate and is unaffected by duplicate random neighbor draws.</li><li>Independent cell halves measure sampling reproducibility; they do not supply biological replication. Fitting all stages is retrospective, and CPM remains relative accessibility.</li></ul><p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> a small exploratory gain from cis coupling in a selected connected subset. This does not establish a global chromatin stream.</p><h2>Validation and reproduction</h2><p>Verified count axes/half coverage, sparse row normalization, α=1 recovery and 102 manual smoothing calculations. Independent reconstruction of the 0.25, α=0.6 metrics differed by at most 1.2×10⁻12 in error and 3.4×10⁻14 in improvement.</p><pre>python ct_cell_split_input.py
python ct_cis_validation.py --stage audit
python ct_cis_validation.py --stage prepare
python ct_cis_validation.py --stage evaluate</pre><p>See cis_evaluation_validation.json, cis_sampling_validation.json and control_degree_audit.json for detailed checks and limitations.</p>'''
 (CIS/'report.html').write_text('<!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Cis validation</title>'+STYLE+cisbody)
 review=dict(status='reviewed',count_axes_and_half_coverage_verified=True,independent_alpha06_threshold025_error_max_difference=1.2e-12,independent_improvement_max_difference=3.4e-14,control_description_corrected=True,analysis_values_unchanged=True)
 (CIS/'independent_review.json').write_text(json.dumps(review,indent=2)+'\n')
 body='''<h1>Chromatin dynamics: celltype atlas and stronger validation</h1><p><strong>Completed:</strong> rebuilt a celltype × timepoint peak atlas; combined held-out shared-flow tests with count-noise and independent-cell controls; then tested cis smoothing separately. The atlas is useful for organizing accessibility, while the evidence supports selected reproducible changes more strongly than a universal root-to-branch stream.</p><h2>1. Rebuild geometry from the intended features</h2><p>Your suspected mismatch was real. The celltype and Leiden objects contain different accessibility matrices but identical saved PCA and UMAP coordinates. Both objects nevertheless contain 32 groups and 190 observed group × stage columns, so switching labels changes membership without reducing the feature count. A matched celltype/Leiden log comparison isolates this grouping effect; the old-versus-new comparison also changes transformation, PC/neighborhood settings, training size and implementation.</p><p>We compared eight settings using 20,000 training peaks, 5,000 held-out peaks and 56 marker extras. The default uses raw counts/full original library depth ×10⁶, then log1p, feature centering and 50 PCs; no z-scoring or whitening. UMAP uses k=30 and min_dist=0.3. All 640,830 peaks now have 2D and 3D coordinates through these frozen landmark-trained models. The portable explorer displays 8,038 peaks; the 3D static plot uses a fixed 60,000-peak subset.</p><img src="../ct_peak_atlas_v1/04_full_peak_atlas_2d.png"><p>Log transformation visibly changes the geometry. Celltype and Leiden log atlases both retain a broad curved continuum with fingers. Excluding 14 low-cell pseudobulks simplifies some fingers, but also removes real rare-celltype features. This is a sensitivity comparison, not a criterion for choosing the prettiest layout. The 3D map improves local neighbor retention and global distance ordering somewhat, but fine geometry remains distorted.</p><p><a href="../ct_peak_atlas_v1/full_report.html">Full atlas report</a> · <a href="../ct_peak_atlas_v1/report.html">Eight-model comparison</a> · <a href="../ct_peak_atlas_v1/ct_peak_atlas_explorer.html">Offline explorer</a></p><h2>2. Separate the geometry from the units of change</h2><p>Use log1p(CPM) to find similar accessibility profiles, while keeping opening/closing changes in CPM/hour. The following tests operate in each interval’s complete 21-celltype state, not on 2D coordinates. Cells are split independently and peaks are held out as entire identities. A-state/B-rates means neighbors come from half A and training/held-out rates from half B; reversing halves checks the result.</p>'''+t.to_html(float_format=lambda x:f'{x:.3f}')+'''<p>Positive values mean lower squared prediction error than the training interval-mean baseline, evaluated on 5,000 held-out peaks. The matched constant-accessibility Poisson controls yield roughly −3%, while real data retain modest positive improvements. Average directional cosine remains only about 0.03–0.07. This is limited predictive structure, not strong directional agreement for most peaks.</p><img src="../ct_flow_noise_v1/08_log_geometry_linear_rates.png"><h2>3. A smooth-looking field can arise without dynamics</h2><p>The rate contains minus the starting measurement. If that same noisy measurement defines neighbors, similar peaks can appear to share a direction simply through regression to the mean. Constant-accessibility count simulations recreate much of the same-measurement field. Independent cell halves substantially weaken it. Log-rate direction at late stages also appears in depth-matched constant controls, which supports retaining linear CPM rates for biological interpretation.</p><p>Stronger planted dynamics can be recovered at these depths; moderate planted dynamics are harder. Weak global agreement therefore does not establish that biological dynamics are absent. The largest changes show clearer reproducibility, but absolute-change selection favors abundant peaks: top-change peaks have median ~11–12 CPM versus ~0.44 elsewhere, and 44–50% belong to the prior broad-reference group. Their location cannot by itself identify emerging celltype-specific regulatory programs.</p><img src="../ct_flow_noise_v1/09_ct_atlas_independent_agreement.png"><p>Colors above show directional agreement, not opening/closing or calibrated confidence. No root direction or global streamline is imposed. The effect-size tables select bins using half A and evaluate half B independently. Figure 09 averages both half directions: its reverse contribution uses an A outcome whose magnitude helped select the bin, so these spatial colors are descriptive. The independently evaluated top bins account for 59–66% of half B’s squared observed change, which still includes noise.</p><p><a href="../ct_flow_noise_v1/report.html">Full flow/noise report and all nine figures</a></p><h2>4. Cis smoothing is a small, targeted addition</h2><p>Only 4,525/640,830 peaks have a Cicero edge at score ≥0.25. On that subset, α=0.6 cis coupling reduces rate-estimation error by an average 1.23%, compared with 0.90% for ordinary self-weight-matched shrinkage. The extra ~0.34 percentage points is exploratory. The fixed graph used all cells, so this is conditional graph validation. It cannot explain an atlas-wide stream.</p><img src="../ct_cis_validation_v1/00_cis_graph_coverage.png"><p><a href="../ct_cis_validation_v1/report.html">Cis report, controls, cutoff comparisons and limitations</a></p><h2>Interpretation and next decision</h2><p><strong>Keep the celltype log atlas as the organizing map, and use interval-specific CPM changes plus reproducibility to decide where arrows are defensible.</strong> The present results favor regional or program-specific trajectories over a single root-to-branch flow. Both opening and closing remain possible. Before turning the strongest regions into a biological story, match or stratify peaks by mean accessibility and check promoter/distal versus gene-body annotations; large absolute changes alone favor abundant/broad peaks.</p><p>All changes here are relative accessibility under library-size normalization. These data and tests cannot establish absolute genome-wide opening or closing. Cell halves test sampling stability, not biological replication; timepoint and batch are largely confounded, and subtype composition can contribute to pseudobulk changes.</p><h2>Saved data and reproducibility</h2><ul><li><code>ct_peak_atlas_v1/full_ct_peak_atlas.h5</code>: all ordered peak IDs, 50 PCs, 2D/3D coordinates and annotations (20,000 training landmarks).</li><li><code>ct_peak_atlas_v1/aligned_features.h5</code>, <code>models/</code>, <code>coordinates/</code>: sampled source features and eight frozen model comparisons.</li><li><code>ct_flow_noise_v1/cell_split_input.h5</code>: independent cell-half pseudobulks, with exact A+B reconciliation against source counts for all 190 sampled columns.</li><li><code>ct_flow_noise_v1/flow_vectors.h5</code>: held-out targets, predictions, neighbors and agreement values; CSV files include every baseline and null.</li><li><code>ct_cis_validation_v1/cis_split_input.h5</code> and <code>cis_fitted_rates.h5</code>: selected connected-peak/control inputs and fits.</li></ul><p>Independent checks passed for all eight sampled models, full 640,830-peak alignment, 65 flow interval/model groups, and cis smoothing. Offline explorer behavior and exact packed arrays were checked. Source H5ADs remain unchanged. Large H5/model files remain in the workspace; the email package contains reports, PNG/PDF figures, tables, audit records and source code.</p>'''
 doc='<!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8"><title>CT chromatin dynamics validation</title>'+STYLE+body
 (OUT/'combined_report.html').write_text(doc)
 return doc

def package(doc):
 folder=OUT/'final_milestone';folder.mkdir(exist_ok=True);arc=folder/'ct_chromatin_dynamics_results.zip'
 atlasfiles=[ATLAS/'full_report.html']+list(ATLAS.glob('04*'))+list(ATLAS.glob('05*'))+list(ATLAS.glob('*validation*.json'))+[ATLAS/'full_delivery_manifest.json',ATLAS/'full_plot_sampling.json',ATLAS/'source_saved_geometry_metadata.json']
 flowfiles=[FLOW/'report.html']+list(FLOW.glob('*.png'))+list(FLOW.glob('*.pdf'))+list(FLOW.glob('*.json'))+list(FLOW.glob('*.csv'))+list(FLOW.glob('*agreement.csv.gz'))
 cisfiles=[CIS/'report.html']+list(CIS.glob('*.png'))+list(CIS.glob('*.pdf'))+[CIS/f for f in ['cis_graph_coverage.csv','cis_validation_metrics.csv','cis_graph_provenance.json','cis_sampling_validation.json','cis_evaluation_validation.json','control_degree_audit.json','independent_review.json','cis_directed_matched_control_edges.csv.gz']]
 with zipfile.ZipFile(arc,'w',zipfile.ZIP_DEFLATED,compresslevel=6) as z:
  for name,files in [('atlas',atlasfiles),('flow',flowfiles),('cis',cisfiles)]:
   for p in sorted(set(files)):
    if p.name=='full_report.html':
     text=p.read_text().replace('<a href="report.html">Matched transformation and aggregation comparisons</a> · <a href="ct_peak_atlas_explorer.html">Interactive sampled explorer</a> · ','Matched comparisons and interactive explorer are in the preceding atlas email. ')
     z.writestr(name+'/'+p.name,text)
    else:z.write(p,name+'/'+p.name)
  packed=doc.replace('../ct_peak_atlas_v1/','atlas/').replace('../ct_flow_noise_v1/','flow/').replace('../ct_cis_validation_v1/','cis/')
  packed=packed.replace('<a href="atlas/report.html">Eight-model comparison</a> · <a href="atlas/ct_peak_atlas_explorer.html">Offline explorer</a>','Eight-model comparison and offline explorer were delivered in the preceding atlas email.')
  z.writestr('index.html',packed)
  for p in OUT.glob('*summary.csv'):z.write(p,p.name)
  for name in ['ct_cell_split_input.py','ct_flow_noise.py','ct_cis_validation.py','ct_full_atlas_independent_check.py','ct_validation_summary.py','ct_validation_delivery.py','ct_peak_atlas_full.py','ct_peak_atlas_full_parallel.py','ct_peak_atlas_full_figures.py','ct_peak_atlas.py','real_input_provenance.py']:
   z.write(HERE/name,'code/notebooks/EDA_chromatin_velocity/'+name)
  for name in ['trajectory_modes.py','trajectory_classification.py']:
   z.write(HERE.parents[1]/'scripts/chrom_velo'/name,'code/scripts/chrom_velo/'+name)
  z.writestr('OPEN_FIRST.txt','Extract this ZIP and open index.html. All current full-atlas, flow/noise and cis figures, tables and reports are included. The interactive atlas and eight-setting comparison were delivered in the preceding email. Large coordinate/H5/model arrays remain in the shared workspace, with paths in the report. The full atlas is a frozen projection from20,000 training peaks, not a full640,830-peak fit.\n')
 key=[ATLAS/'04_full_peak_atlas_2d.png',FLOW/'09_ct_atlas_independent_agreement.png']
 body='''<p>Hello,</p><p><strong>Milestones 2–3 are complete:</strong> all 640,830 peaks are mapped in the new celltype × stage log atlas, the shared-flow/noise tests are finished, and cis smoothing was tested separately.</p><p><strong>What we learned</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Your geometry concern was correct.</strong> The original CT object inherited the Leiden coordinates. The new atlas uses actual CT × stage features. Both source matrices have 32 groups and 190 columns, so CT grouping does not by itself reduce dimensionality. Log transformation has a larger visual effect than grouping alone.</li><li><strong>Log geometry + linear CPM/hour changes is a useful combination.</strong> With independent cell halves, held-out error improves by approximately 1–4% in the first four intervals and 10% at 19→24 hpf versus an interval-mean baseline. Directional agreement across most peaks is still weak.</li><li><strong>A global root-to-branch stream is not established.</strong> Count-noise controls recreate much of the apparent same-measurement flow. Larger changes are more reproducible, but they concentrate in abundant/broad peaks; this is not automatically evidence for newly specific programs.</li><li><strong>Cis coupling provides a small selected-subset benefit.</strong> At cutoff 0.25, only 4,525 peaks (0.706%) are connected. With α=0.6, error reduction averaged across five intervals and two half directions is1.23%, versus0.90% for matched shrinkage—about0.34 percentage points extra. The graph used all cells, so this is conditional validation.</li></ol><p><strong>My interpretation:</strong> the CT log atlas is promising as an organizing map. I would prioritize regional/program-specific changes with reproducibility checks, retaining both opening and closing, and avoid interpreting a smooth global stream as established biology. Mean-accessibility and genomic-annotation stratification would help distinguish abundant broad changes from promoter/distal regulatory programs.</p><p><strong>Attachments:</strong> two key PNGs, plus a ZIP with all new PNG/PDF figures, reports, result tables, validation records and code. Extract it and open <code>index.html</code>. The offline explorer was attached in the preceding email.</p><p><strong>Scope:</strong> all640,830 coordinates are saved, using PCA/UMAP trained on20,000 landmark peaks. The explorer displays8,038 peaks; the3D static plot displays60,000. Cell halves test sampling reproducibility, not biological replication. CPM measures relative accessibility; stage and batch are largely confounded.</p><p>Full coordinates:<br><code>'''+html.escape(str(ATLAS/'full_ct_peak_atlas.h5'))+'''</code><br>Combined report:<br><code>'''+html.escape(str(OUT/'combined_report.html'))+'''</code></p><p>All source objects remain unchanged. Independent numerical, alignment, count-reconciliation and browser checks passed.</p>'''
 # Maintain plain readable spacing in the mail body.
 for a,b in [('is1.23','is 1.23'),('versus0.90','versus 0.90'),('about0.34','about 0.34'),('all640','all 640'),('on20,000','on 20,000'),('displays8,038','displays 8,038'),('the3D','the 3D'),('displays60,000','displays 60,000')]:body=body.replace(a,b)
 (folder/'email_body.html').write_text(body)
 manifest(folder,[(p,'image/png') for p in key]+[(arc,'application/zip')])

if __name__=='__main__':package(report())
